  "preamble": "Laxing of the close front vowel [i] to the near-close near-front [ɪ]. Middle English short /i/ became the lax /ɪ/ of Modern English kit (fisch /fiʃ/ > fish /fɪʃ/); Roger Lass dates the laxing to the 17th century, other scholars earlier. In Ukrainian, Old East Slavic и [i] merged with ы [ɨ] as [ɪ] (бити > бити [ˈbɪte] 'to beat'). In Central Javanese, /i/ is pronounced [ɪ] in closed syllables (mari [mari] 'to recover' but wit [wɪt] 'seedling').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Laxing, Lowering",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Middle English to Modern English",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Middle English fisch /fiʃ/ 'fish'",
          "to": "fish /fɪʃ/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Middle English hil /hil/ 'hill'",
          "to": "hill /hɪl/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Middle English drynken /ˈdrinkən/ 'to drink'",
          "to": "drink /ˈdɹɪŋk/",
          "note": "Short /u/ was laxed to /ʊ/ in parallel (pullen > pull)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Ukrainian",
      "languageFamily": "East Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old East Slavic бити /ˈbʲitʲi/ 'to beat' (11th century)",
          "to": "бити [ˈbɪte]"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old East Slavic сила 'strength'",
          "to": "сила [ˈsɪɫɐ]"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *lipa 'linden'",
          "to": "липа [ˈɫɪpɐ]",
          "note": "Old и [i] merged with ы [ɨ] as и [ɪ], and the preceding consonant lost its palatalization; Ukrainian [i], written і, comes from other sources."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
